\section{Literature and context check}
Ishai's FSS talk in OWR 5/2017, p.~236, was read. It defines additive function shares, privacy against every strict subset of parties, and asks specifically about efficient one-way-function-based point-function FSS for at least three parties. The source already compares with truth-table sharing. The following information-theoretic lower bound investigates why replacing computational privacy by perfect privacy is not a free simplification; it does not characterize one-way-function constructions.
\section{Research attempt: Perfect-privacy point sharing requires long additive keys}

\paragraph{A baseline with exact privacy.}
Let the domain have $N$ points, and represent a Boolean function by its truth table $F\in\mathbb F_2^N$. For $m\ge2$, choose independent uniform vectors $K_1,\ldots,K_{m-1}\in\mathbb F_2^N$ and set $K_m=F+\sum_{j<m}K_j$. Party $j$ outputs the requested coordinate of $K_j$. Their sum is $F$ at every point. Every strict coalition sees a uniform tuple independent of $F$: if it omits party $m$, this is immediate; otherwise at least one independent uniform mask is missing, and it makes $K_m$ uniform conditional on all the observed masks. Each key has $N$ bits.

\paragraph{A near-matching lower bound even for point functions.}
Now restrict the hidden function to $F=e_i$, the point function at $i\in[N]$, where $N\ge2$. Consider any $m$-party scheme with the following additional hypotheses: local evaluations are deterministic functions of a key and the public input; outputs add in $\mathbb F_2$; correctness holds with probability one simultaneously at all $N$ points; and every strict coalition's key distribution is exactly independent of $i$. Let $V_j\in\mathbb F_2^N$ be the full evaluation vector determined by party $j$'s key.

Fix a party $j$. Let $B=V_j$ and $A=\sum_{\ell\ne j}V_\ell$. The laws of $A$ and of $B$ are each independent of the selected point by the two relevant coalition privacy conditions, and correctness gives $B=e_i+A$. Denote the common law of $A$ by $\mu$. Since the law of $B$ is the same for every $i$, the measure $\mu$ is invariant under translation by every vector $e_i+e_{i'}$. These vectors span the even-parity subspace
\[
 E=\{v\in\mathbb F_2^N:\sum_i v_i=0\},\qquad |E|=2^{N-1}.
\]
Indeed $e_1+e_i$ for $i>1$ are linearly independent and span that subspace: the last $N-1$ coordinates determine every even-parity vector.

Translation invariance forces $\mu$ to be uniform within each of the two cosets of $E$. Conditional on its parity, $A$ therefore has entropy $N-1$ bits; hence $H(A)\ge N-1$. The same holds for $B=e_i+A$. Since $B$ is a deterministic function of party $j$'s key, that key must have entropy at least $N-1$: grouping key outcomes by their evaluation vector cannot increase entropy, as follows directly by decomposing entropy into the entropy of the group label plus the nonnegative conditional entropy. In particular, if each key is encoded in at most $\ell$ bits in a fixed-length encoding, $\ell\ge N-1$. The argument holds for every party and every $m\ge2$, including three or more parties.

\paragraph{Why this does not refute succinct computational FSS.}
The proof uses equality of distributions to propagate invariance along all translations in a subspace of exponential size. Computational indistinguishability is weaker and does not imply equality or uniformity on those cosets. An unbounded observer could enumerate the truth table and distinguish distributions that efficient observers cannot distinguish. Thus the lower bound applies to the explicitly stronger perfect-privacy model, while the source asks for computational constructions from one-way functions. Nor does it establish that one-way functions are necessary for every useful FSS instance: that would need a separate reduction with the source's exact functionality, security and efficiency parameters.
\section{Verification and adversarial audit}
The baseline coalition proof handles coalitions containing the final balancing share. The lower bound uses privacy of the single selected party and of its complement; it does not mistakenly assume independent shares. The even-parity span, both cosets, arbitrary party count, $N=2$, and the entropy-to-fixed-key-length implication were checked. For $N=1$ the stated lower bound is zero and the nontrivial span argument is unnecessary.
\paragraph{Audit corrections.} Replaced a weak counting argument based only on the $N$ possible point functions by a proved translation-invariance argument. Kept perfect privacy, deterministic evaluation, exact correctness and fixed-length key encoding explicit; none is silently imposed on the original computational target.
\paragraph{External computation.} None; symbolic arguments and boundary checks only.
\section{Scope and remaining gaps}
No improved one-way-function-based scheme, full function-class characterization, or necessary-and-sufficient cryptographic assumption theorem is supplied. The actual result is an elementary perfect-privacy obstruction explaining why a tempting information-theoretic replacement cannot give short point-function keys. Computational privacy remains the essential missing ingredient.
